==================================================
Lab 3: Server-Side Template Injection Using Documentation (Freemarker)
==================================================

=======================
WHAT THIS LAB IS ABOUT
=======================

A shopping website lets a content manager edit the template that generates each product description.
The template is processed by the Freemarker engine, so anything we write in it runs on the server.
The Freemarker documentation describes a built-in that can create Java objects, and one of those objects runs shell commands.

Goal: delete /home/carlos/morale.txt by executing a system command through the template.

==============================
HOW THE WEBSITE NORMALLY WORKS
==============================

A content manager logs in, opens the template editor for a product, and saves new template text.
The product page then renders that template and fills in values such as ${product.name} and ${product.stock}.

=======================
 WHAT IS THE PROBLEM
=======================

The template text is treated as code, not as plain page content.
Anyone who can edit the template can use Freemarker's syntax to call Java classes on the server.

===========================
THREE THINGS TO UNDERSTAND
===========================

Freemarker expression (${ ... }): Evaluates the expression inside and prints the result. The lab's error message showed that Freemarker was in use.
new() built-in: Creates an object of a named Java class. The Freemarker documentation warns that this is dangerous when users control templates.
Execute class: A class in freemarker.template.utility that implements TemplateModel and runs a shell command when called.

======================================
THE ATTACK
======================================

We save this as the template:

<#assign ex="freemarker.template.utility.Execute"?new()> ${ ex("rm /home/carlos/morale.txt") }

The first part creates an Execute object and stores it in the variable ex.
The second part calls it with the command, so the server runs rm on Carlos's file.
The output of the command is not needed. The lab checks only that the file is deleted.

============================
HOW TO SOLVE IT MANUALLY
============================

Log in as content-manager and open a product's template editor.
Replace the template text with the payload above and click Save template.
Open the product page. The lab is marked as solved once the file is deleted.

=========================
WHAT THIS SCRIPT DOES
=========================

* It logs in as content-manager and checks that the login worked.
* It loads /product/template?productId=2 and reads the CSRF token from that page, since the template form uses its own token.
* It posts the payload with the same fields the browser sends: csrf, template, and template-action=save.
* It loads the product page so Freemarker evaluates the saved template.

=============================
WHY THIS HAPPENS
=============================

The application lets users write templates that the server evaluates.
Once a template can create arbitrary Java objects, it can call any method those objects expose, including system commands.

=============================
HOW TO PREVENT THIS
=============================

Do not let users edit template source. Let them change only the data that a fixed template displays.
If templates must be user-editable, use a sandboxed configuration that disables new() and the Execute class.
Run the application with the lowest permissions it needs, so a command cannot touch sensitive files.

=============================
 KEY POINTS TO REMEMBER
=============================
A template engine's documentation can reveal the exact object needed for code execution.
Read the real request in the browser or Burp before writing a script, since the required fields may differ from the form you see.
Never let users control template source.
